PLACES=[
N('central-mine-keweenaw','Central Mine and village','Central, Houghton Township','mi','town','ruins',47.40577,-88.20042,'site','public','walk','','1854–1898',
  'A copper mining town in the Keweenaw. The Central mine opened in 1854 and produced nearly 52 million pounds of copper before it closed in 1898; the company had built about 130 structures for the mine and its workers. The town emptied after the closing. The Keweenaw County Historical Society bought 38 acres of the site in 1996 and is restoring some of the houses.',
  'About 20 frame buildings of the original 130 still stand, most of them two-storey company houses on stone foundations, with scattered ruins between them. Two short trails (a quarter mile and a half mile) pass house foundations and the school site and climb the upper rock pile for a view of the valley. The Methodist Episcopal church of 1868–69, with its battlemented tower, is in nearly original condition and opens once a year, on the last Sunday in July, for the Central Reunion. The visitor center has exhibits and a video, and House 61 is shown furnished.',
  'A copper town that emptied in 1898 and whose families have come back to its church every July since 1907.',
  'Keweenaw County Historical Society land, about 5 miles northeast of Phoenix just off U.S. 41 at the Central–Gratiot Lake Road; visitor center at 7143 Central Road. The society says the mine site is open all year; the visitor center is open in summer, with no admission fee (donations asked). Some houses are lived in: keep away from occupied dwellings. Lot beside the visitor center.',
  [W('Central Mine Historic District'),W('Central Mine Methodist Church'),['Keweenaw County Historical Society: Central Mine','https://keweenawhistory.org/Central'],KCHS],
  th=[47.40611,-88.20068],thn='Visitor center lot, Central Road',brick='?',
  hours='Visitor center June 20 to October 10, daily 9 a.m. to 5 p.m. (2027 season); the mine site is open all year'),
N('quincy-mine-hoist-house','Quincy Mine No. 2 Shaft Hoist House','Hancock','mi','industry','standing',47.1357,-88.5731,'exact','open','walk','','1918–1929',
  'The Quincy Mining Company worked copper on the hill above Hancock from 1856 and paid dividends every year from 1867 to 1921. As its shafts went past 7,000 feet, it built this reinforced-concrete hoist house in 1918 for a Nordberg steam hoist, the largest steam hoisting engine in the world, on the largest concrete engine foundation ever poured. The building and hoist cost over $370,000 and were used for only eleven years, until 1929.',
  'The 1918 hoist house, a concrete building with brick veneer and tiled walls, still holds the Nordberg hoist. The Quincy Mine Hoist Association gives guided tours of the hoist house and of the mine\'s seventh level, reached by the cog railway down to an adit; self-guided tours of the surface landscape are free. The famous No. 6 shaft house is no longer standing.',
  'The largest steam hoist ever built, used for eleven years.',
  'Run by the Quincy Mine Hoist Association, 49750 U.S. 41, Hancock, a heritage site of Keweenaw National Historical Park. Tours are paid; the association advises booking ahead because walk-in tours may be full. Hours from the association\'s home page (they may vary in bad weather). Parking off U.S. 41 by the hoist house.',
  [W('Quincy Mine No. 2 Shaft Hoist House'),W('Quincy Mine'),['Quincy Mine Hoist Association','https://quincymine.com']],
  th=[47.13695,-88.57485],thn='Lot off U.S. 41',brick='?',
  hours='May to October daily 8:30 a.m. to 5 p.m.; November to April Monday to Saturday 10 a.m. to 4 p.m.'),
N('eagle-harbor-light','Eagle Harbor Light Station','Eagle Harbor','mi','light','standing',47.45972,-88.15917,'exact','open','drive','','1871',
  'The first light at Eagle Harbor was built in 1851. It was replaced in 1871 by the present red brick lighthouse, a keeper\'s house with its light standing on high ground 47 feet above the lake.',
  'The 1871 red brick lighthouse is shown as a museum by the Keweenaw County Historical Society, with a maritime museum in the old fog signal building, a commercial fishing museum, a history museum and an exhibit on the 1926 wreck of the City of Bangor. One admission covers the whole complex.',
  '',
  'Keweenaw County Historical Society, 670 Lighthouse Road, Eagle Harbor. Admission $8 for the complex, under 16 free (society\'s schedule). Parking at the light station was not checked on the ground.',
  [W('Eagle Harbor Light'),KCHS],
  brick='yes',
  hours='June 20 to October 10 (2027): July and August 10 a.m. to 5 p.m. Monday to Saturday, noon to 5 p.m. Sunday; June, September and October noon to 5 p.m.'),
N('calumet-theatre','Calumet Theatre','Calumet','mi','civic','standing',47.24776,-88.45363,'exact','open','drive','','1900',
  'Built by the village of Calumet at the height of the copper boom and opened on March 20, 1900, with an operetta on tour from Broadway. The theatre says it was the first theatre in the United States built and owned by a municipality. Sarah Bernhardt, John Philip Sousa and Douglas Fairbanks Sr. played its stage.',
  'A two-storey Renaissance Revival building of yellowish-brown brick on a Jacobsville sandstone foundation, with a copper roof and cornices and a clock tower beside it. Inside, the auditorium was restored in 1975 to the crimson, gold and ivory scheme of the Chicago designer William Eckert, and the exterior in 1988–89. Two-hour guided tours take in the stage, the auditorium and the Red Jacket Ballroom, with about three flights of stairs.',
  'A copper town\'s own opera house, still working.',
  'The Calumet Theatre, in downtown Calumet; still a working theatre. Paid tours; children under 6 free. Tours can also be arranged through the box office, (906) 337-2610. Street parking downtown, not checked on the ground.',
  [W('The Calumet Theatre'),['Calumet Theatre: guided tours','https://www.calumettheatre.com/?p=9749']],
  brick='no',
  hours='Guided tours Wednesday to Friday at 1 p.m., or at other times by arrangement with the box office'),
N('fort-wilkins-copper-harbor','Fort Wilkins','Copper Harbor','mi','fort','standing',47.46642,-87.86546,'site','open','walk','','1844–1870',
  'The Army built Fort Wilkins on the strait between Copper Harbor and Lake Fanny Hooe in 1844, during the copper rush, to keep order between miners and the Ojibwe. It proved unnecessary: the garrison left for the Mexican War in 1846, leaving one caretaker sergeant, and after a second garrison from 1867 to 1870 the Army left for good. It became a state park in 1923.',
  'The Michigan History Center counts 19 buildings, 12 of them original log and frame structures of the 1840s; the others were rebuilt after archaeological digs. In summer costumed staff portray the fort\'s last summer as an active post, with museum exhibits in the buildings. The park also holds the 1866 Copper Harbor lighthouse.',
  '',
  'Fort Wilkins Historic State Park, Michigan Department of Natural Resources, on U.S. 41 just east of Copper Harbor. Admission to the fort is free; a Recreation Passport is needed for vehicle entry. Open in summer; the History Center\'s page gave no dates or times when this was written (the fort was closed for the season).',
  [W('Fort Wilkins Historic State Park'),['Michigan History Center: Fort Wilkins and Copper Harbor Lighthouses','https://michigan.gov/mhc/museums/fwchl']],
  th=[47.46739,-87.86631],thn='Park lot off U.S. 41 (OpenStreetMap)',brick='no',
  hours='?'),
N('bay-furnace-onota','Bay Furnace (Onota)','Au Train Township','mi','industry','ruins',46.440833,-86.705833,'site','public','walk','','1869–1877',
  'A charcoal blast furnace built in 1869–70 on Grand Island Bay, with the village of Onota round it. By 1874 its stack was making fifteen tons of pig iron a day, fed by 52 charcoal kilns in the company\'s timber, and ore and iron came and went by a pier some 1,400 feet long. The company was bankrupt by 1876, and in 1877 fire destroyed the village, which was not rebuilt.',
  'The coursed-stone ruin of Stack No. 1, partly rebuilt on its original spot, is all that is left of Onota. A second stack, an iron shell on columns, was used for only a year and is gone.',
  'An iron town that burned in 1877 and left only its furnace.',
  'Hiawatha National Forest: the Bay Furnace campground and day-use area on M-28, about five miles west of Munising near Christmas. The Forest Service lists the campground season as May 9 to October 8; the day-use area has picnic sites and a rocky beach.',
  [W('Onota, Au Train Township, Michigan'),['Hiawatha National Forest: Bay Furnace Campground','https://www.fs.usda.gov/r09/hiawatha/recreation/bay-furnace-campground']],
  brick='no'),
N('whitefish-point-light','Whitefish Point Light Station','Whitefish Point','mi','light','standing',46.77111,-84.9575,'site','open','drive','','1861',
  'A light was first built at Whitefish Point in 1849, on the stretch of Lake Superior shore called the Graveyard of the Great Lakes, where every ship entering or leaving the lake passes. The present iron skeleton tower dates from 1861, framed to stand up to the wind. It is the oldest working lighthouse in the Upper Peninsula.',
  'The 1861 tower, the 1861 keeper\'s quarters and the 1923 Coast Guard surfboat house are part of the Great Lakes Shipwreck Museum, whose main gallery holds the bell of the Edmund Fitzgerald. Self-guided tours of the buildings come with admission.',
  'The light at the Graveyard of the Great Lakes, and the Fitzgerald\'s bell.',
  'Great Lakes Shipwreck Historical Society, at the end of Whitefish Point Road north of Paradise. Paid admission (children 17 and under $11). Parking at the museum.',
  [W('Whitefish Point Light'),['Great Lakes Shipwreck Museum: admission, hours and location','https://shipwreckmuseum.com/visit/admission-hours-location/']],
  th=[46.77072,-84.95725],thn='Museum lot, Whitefish Point Road',brick='?',
  hours='May 1 to October 31, daily 9 a.m. to 5 p.m.'),
N('point-iroquois-light','Point Iroquois Light Station','Brimley','mi','light','standing',46.48428,-84.63234,'exact','open','drive','','1870',
  'The light on Point Iroquois marks where Whitefish Bay narrows into the St. Marys River. A rubble-stone light of 1855–56 was torn down and replaced in 1870 by the present white brick tower and keeper\'s house, which ran for 93 years. A wing for another keeper was added in 1905.',
  'The 65-foot white brick tower and keepers\' house stand on the bluff; the fog signal building is gone. Inside are a museum with a fourth-order Fresnel lens from Martin Reef Light and one apartment furnished as the keepers lived; the tower can be climbed.',
  '',
  'Hiawatha National Forest, on West Lakeshore Drive west of Brimley. The Forest Service lists the tower, museum and season below; the Bay Mills Indian Community exhibit was closed for renovation. Lot on Lakeshore Drive.',
  [W('Point Iroquois Light'),['Hiawatha National Forest: Point Iroquois Lighthouse','https://www.fs.usda.gov/r09/hiawatha/recreation/point-iroquois-lighthouse']],
  th=[46.48374,-84.63172],thn='Lot on West Lakeshore Drive (OpenStreetMap)',brick='no',
  hours='June 9 to October 16: museum and tower Tuesday to Saturday 11 a.m. to 4 p.m.'),
N('fort-mackinac','Fort Mackinac','Mackinac Island','mi','fort','standing',45.85222,-84.61722,'exact','open','walk','','1780–1895',
  'The British built a limestone fort on the bluff of Mackinac Island in 1780–81, during the Revolution, to command the Straits of Mackinac and the fur trade. It changed hands in the War of 1812 and served the U.S. Army until it closed in 1895; from 1875 to 1895 it was headquarters of Mackinac National Park. It is now a museum in Mackinac Island State Park.',
  'The British-built stone walls and fourteen buildings, all restored and open. Costumed interpreters give demonstrations, tours and cannon salutes through the day; a curator leads a 90-minute tour.',
  '',
  'Mackinac State Historic Parks. The island has no cars: reach it by ferry from Mackinaw City or St. Ignace, then walk or cycle up. Enter only by the North Sally Port. Adults $18, children 5 to 12 $10.75 (2027); admission includes several other island museums.',
  [W('Fort Mackinac'),MSHP_FM],
  brick='?',
  hours='April 30 to October 23 (2027); June 5 to September 5 daily 9:30 a.m. to 7 p.m., September 6 to October 10 9:30 a.m. to 5 p.m.'),
N('colonial-michilimackinac','Colonial Michilimackinac','Mackinaw City','mi','fort','standing',45.78667,-84.73598,'site','open','walk','','1715–1783',
  'A French fort and trading post built about 1715 at the tip of the Lower Peninsula, later British. The British moved to Mackinac Island in 1781–83, carrying buildings across the water and the ice, and the remains were burned. The palisade was rebuilt in 1933, and archaeologists have found at least 40 structures inside the walls, of which two-thirds have been rebuilt.',
  'A reconstruction on the original site: the palisade and sixteen buildings, furnished or with exhibits, all open. From early June to mid-August archaeologists can be watched digging inside the fort.',
  'A fort rebuilt from what the archaeologists find, with the digging still going on.',
  'Mackinac State Historic Parks, beside the Mackinac Bridge in Mackinaw City. Adults $18, children 5 to 12 $10.75 (2027). Parking is free; the main lot is by the visitor center.',
  [W('Fort Michilimackinac'),MSHP_CM],
  th=[45.78660,-84.73368],thn='Visitor center lot (OpenStreetMap)',brick='no',
  hours='June 5 to September 5 daily 9 a.m. to 7 p.m.; September 6 to October 7 9 a.m. to 5 p.m. (2027); earlier spring days not checked'),
N('cornish-pump-iron-mountain','Chapin Mine Cornish Pump','Iron Mountain','mi','industry','standing',45.82492,-88.07032,'exact','open','drive','','1890–91',
  'The Chapin mine was the richest of the Menominee iron range, but part of its ore lay under a cedar swamp and water was a constant trouble. In 1890–91 E. P. Allis of Milwaukee built a steam "Cornish" pump for it, designed by Edwin P. Reynolds; the giant engine started on January 3, 1893. It is the largest reciprocating steam engine ever built in the United States.',
  'The pump engine and its flywheel stand in a museum that also shows underground mining equipment: ore cars, drills, skips, a man car, a dynamite car and a steam boiler, with more large machines outside. Wikipedia describes the original pump house as a 60-foot red sandstone building; whether the museum building is that house was not checked.',
  '',
  'Menominee Range Historical Foundation, at Kent Street and Kimberly Avenue, Iron Mountain. Seasonal; telephone (906) 774-1086. The foundation\'s page said closed for the season and gave no summer hours when this was written.',
  [W('Chapin Mine Steam Pump Engine'),['Menominee Range Historical Foundation: Cornish Pump Museum','https://menomineemuseum.com/cornish-pump-museum']],
  brick='?',
  hours='?'),
N('marlborough-cement-ruins','Marlborough cement plant ruins','Pleasant Plains Township','mi','industry','ruins',43.861111,-85.838056,'area','view','drive','','1890s–1906',
  'In the 1890s the Great Northern Portland Cement Company built a plant here to make cement from the local marl, and from 1902 a company village, Marlborough, which had 400 people by 1905. The cement was poor and the plant needed so much power that the company built the largest power plant in the Lower Peninsula. It went into receivership in 1906; the houses were sold for salvage and the plant was dynamited for scrap iron.',
  'Reinforced-concrete ruins of the plant spread over about 80 acres; a radio station\'s write-up says ruins stand alongside James Road and others are spread through the woods. Wikipedia says none of the village\'s 72 houses, opera house, school or 88-room hotel remain; the same radio page says some do stand in ruins. The two disagree and neither was checked on the ground.',
  'A concrete company town dynamited for scrap within ten years.',
  'Ownership not found: the federal public-land map does not show the site, and the radio station\'s page warns of trespassing on private property. Look from James Road only. No parking was found.',
  [W('Marlborough Historic District'),['99.1 WFMK: Marlborough ruins','https://99wfmk.com/marlboroughruins/']],
  conf='mixed',brick='no'),
N('fort-gratiot-light','Fort Gratiot Light Station','Port Huron','mi','light','standing',43.00631,-82.42247,'exact','open','drive','','1829',
  'The first lighthouse in Michigan was built here in 1825 at the mouth of the St. Clair River; its tower fell in 1828, and Congress paid for a new 65-foot tower in 1829. It was raised to 82 feet in 1862, and a brick duplex keeper\'s house was added in 1874. It is the oldest surviving lighthouse in Michigan and still works. The Coast Guard closed it in 2008 for falling brickwork; St. Clair County took it over in 2010 and tours began again in 2012.',
  'The tower, keeper\'s dwelling and Post Hospital are shown by Port Huron Museums; the tower can be climbed only on the guided tour, and not in severe weather. The museums report renovation work on the keeper\'s dwelling and grounds.',
  '',
  'St. Clair County Parks and Recreation, run with Port Huron Museums, 2802 Omar Street, Port Huron. Adults $12; groups all year by appointment. Lighthouse Park lot beside the station.',
  [W('Fort Gratiot Light'),['Port Huron Museums: Fort Gratiot Lighthouse','https://www.phmuseum.org/fort-gratiot-lighthouse/']],
  th=[43.00555,-82.42361],thn='Lighthouse Park lot, Conger Street',brick='?',
  hours='September 12 to December 20, 2026: Saturday and Sunday 10 a.m. to 5 p.m., last tour 4 p.m.; spring and summer hours not checked'),
N('michigan-central-station','Michigan Central Station','Detroit','mi','rail','standing',42.3288,-83.07773,'exact','open','walk','','1913–1988',
  'Detroit\'s main railway station, Beaux-Arts, by architects who had worked on Grand Central Terminal, with a thirteen-storey office tower; the tallest rail station in the world when built. It went into service early, in 1913, after the old depot burned, and closed when Amtrak left on January 6, 1988. For thirty years it stood empty. Ford bought it in 2018, spent more than $900 million restoring it, and reopened it on June 6, 2024.',
  'The restored waiting room and concourse: 29,000 Guastavino ceiling tiles, marble floors and restored brickwork. The ground floor is open to the public, with shops; guided 90-minute ground-floor tours are run by Detroit History Tours and should be booked.',
  'The ruin that stood for Detroit for thirty years, restored.',
  'Owned by Ford\'s Michigan Central, in Corktown. Lobby hours from Michigan Central\'s visit page; tours are paid. Parking in the Bagley Mobility Hub garage ($5 a half hour, $15 a day, cashless); side-street rules are strictly enforced.',
  [W('Michigan Central Station'),['Michigan Central: tours and visitor information','https://michigancentral.com/visit/']],
  brick='?',
  hours='Lobby daily 8 a.m. to 5 p.m.; guided tours by booking'),
N('historic-fort-wayne-detroit','Historic Fort Wayne','Detroit','mi','fort','standing',42.29917,-83.09722,'site','open','walk','','1845',
  'A star fort on the Detroit River, built in 1845 on a sand hill where the river is under half a mile from Ontario, on one of about 19 Native American burial mounds that once stood nearby. Its earthen ramparts were refaced in brick from 1863. The Army kept building on the post, adding a hospital in 1890 and barracks, a service club and headquarters soon after; since the 1970s the City of Detroit has run 83 acres of it.',
  'The bastioned fort with vaulted brick casemates, the 1848 limestone barracks with brick additions of 1861, and a limestone powder magazine. Outside the fort stand officers\' quarters, a theatre, commissary, guard house and stables; the post hospital was torn down in 2007. Interiors are seen on tours.',
  '',
  'City of Detroit, 6325 West Jefferson Avenue at the foot of Livernois. Free parking in the visitors\' lot. City tours are booked through the city\'s scheduling page; the Historic Fort Wayne Coalition gives guided tours of about two hours on select Saturdays.',
  [W('Fort Wayne (Detroit)'),['City of Detroit: Historic Fort Wayne, visit','https://historicfortwayne.org/visit'],['Historic Fort Wayne Coalition: hours and directions','https://historicfortwaynecoalition.com/hours_directions.html']],
  brick='?',
  hours='Grounds daily 6 a.m. to 8 p.m.; coalition tours select Saturdays at 1 p.m., May to October'),
N('honolulu-house-marshall','Honolulu House','Marshall','mi','landmark','standing',42.27269,-84.96456,'exact','open','drive','','1860',
  'Built in 1860 by Abner Pratt, a former chief justice of the Michigan Supreme Court who had been U.S. consul to Hawaii, to recall the islands where he had lived. It mixes Italianate and Gothic Revival with Polynesian touches, and Pratt had the walls painted with tropical scenes; a later owner repainted them in high Victorian style in 1887. The Marshall Historical Society has kept it as a museum since the 1960s.',
  'A nine-bay porch with a pagoda-topped tower over the centre, a raised veranda, board-and-batten siding and pointed-arch doors, painted in its 1885 colours. Inside, the ceiling and wall paintings have been restored. It stands in Marshall\'s National Historic Landmark District.',
  'A Michigan judge\'s Hawaiian house.',
  'Marshall Historical Society, 107 North Kalamazoo Avenue at Mansion Street. Adults $10, children 12 and under free; candlelight tours in the autumn. Street parking, not checked on the ground.',
  [W('Honolulu House'),['Marshall Historical Society: Honolulu House','https://www.marshallhistoricalsociety.com/?p=391']],
  brick='no',
  hours='May to December: Thursday to Sunday noon to 4 p.m.'),
N('river-raisin-battlefield','River Raisin National Battlefield Park','Monroe','mi','fort','traces',41.91361,-83.37833,'area','public','walk','','1813',
  'The battlefield of Frenchtown, fought in January 1813 on the River Raisin, where many of the American troops were inexperienced Kentuckians; the killing of wounded prisoners afterward became known as the River Raisin Massacre. It is the only national battlefield park for the War of 1812. A paper mill stood on the ground from about 1911 to 1995; it was torn down by 2009 and the land passed to the National Park Service in 2011.',
  'Open ground where the battle was fought, with a visitor center holding a diorama of the River Raisin settlement and a few relics found by archaeologists. A trail links the park with Sterling State Park.',
  '',
  'National Park Service, in Monroe. Grounds open all year from sunrise to sunset; the park\'s hours page says the visitor center is open but gives no times. Parking was not checked on the ground.',
  [W('River Raisin National Battlefield Park'),['National Park Service: River Raisin operating hours','https://www.nps.gov/rira/planyourvisit/hours.htm']],
  brick='no',
  hours='Grounds all year, sunrise to sunset'),
N('castle-museum-saginaw','Castle Museum (Saginaw post office)','Saginaw','mi','civic','standing',43.4311,-83.9355,'exact','open','drive','','1898',
  'Built in 1898 as Saginaw\'s post office, designed by William Martin Aiken, Supervising Architect of the Treasury, as a French chateau to recall the valley\'s first French fur traders. Slated for demolition in 1935, it was instead enlarged with wings of stone from the same quarry, losing one of its three turrets. Saved again when a new post office was built in the 1970s, it is now the museum of the Historical Society of Saginaw County.',
  'A chateau-style stone post office with its turrets and the 1930s wings. Inside, changing exhibits on Saginaw history; interior fabric not checked.',
  'A post office built as a castle, saved twice.',
  'Historical Society of Saginaw County, 500 Federal Avenue, Saginaw. Free for Saginaw County residents; $3 a person or $5 a family otherwise. Closed on major holidays. Directions and parking on the museum\'s site.',
  [W('Castle Museum of Saginaw County History'),['Castle Museum: hours and admission','https://www.castlemuseum.org/hours-admission']],
  brick='no',
  hours='Monday to Wednesday, Friday and Saturday 10 a.m. to 4:30 p.m.; Thursday 10 a.m. to 7 p.m.'),
]

